WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.410
Film Courage:&nbsp; With YOUNG ADULT fiction,
one of the tests should be, is it enjoyable

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.540
to adults.&nbsp;

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.170
And that if it’s not [enjoyable], maybe
a Young Adult readership would be bored because

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.451
it’s not sophisticated enough?

00:00:11.451 --> 00:00:13.450
Jennifer Brody: &nbsp; That is a really great
question.&nbsp;

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:20.680
I would say that kids tend to read up which
means that whatever the age range that we

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.050
specify for these books, I find that they
tend to read up.&nbsp;

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.090
I was reading Stephen King and Anne Rice when
I was in middle school and high school.&nbsp;

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.230
But I probably started out reading things
like Judy Blume and things that skewed a little

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.230
bit younger.&nbsp;

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.899
I think it does need to have a certain level
of sophistication to hold a teen audience

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:39.899
now.&nbsp;

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:44.672
If it skews too old…I mean my books are
considered more cross-over for some of the

00:00:44.672 --> 00:00:46.500
reasons I was talking about.&nbsp;

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.670
I have some adult points-of-view.&nbsp;

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.739
That it is a third-person, multiple points-of-view
story.&nbsp;

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:56.269
So when I was originally trying to sell it,
we got a lot of feedback like this is cross-over,

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:58.399
we don’t know how to market it.&nbsp;

00:00:58.399 --> 00:00:59.949
Isn’t that a good problem?&nbsp;

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:03.799
Wouldn’t it be better if it appeals to both
teens and adults?

00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:07.840
&nbsp; So the publishing industry has been a little
slow to catch up this idea that a lot of adults

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.930
are actually reading young adults and that
teen readers do tend to read up.&nbsp;

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.950
So I think they’re a little behind in understanding
that.&nbsp;

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.990
But I think it is to the benefit if the book
is a little more sophisticated and a little

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.369
more appealing to teens and adults.&nbsp;

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:26.240
But yes, I think you need to keep their attention.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.950
Another thing that I think is important in
which we really tried to do with my series

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.380
is to put the books out in a relatively quick
timeframe.&nbsp;

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.990
Because also readers will grow out of reading
something.&nbsp;

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.560
So if you have multiple years between books
coming out, I think you’re doing yourself

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.390
a disservice because by the time the second
book comes out [your YA readership] they’re

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.590
going to be in college and then the third
book, who knows?&nbsp;

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:52.490
So we’re trying to do six months apart on
my trilogy because I think that makes a lot

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.490
of sense.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:02:00.039
Film Courage:&nbsp; Is there a focus group that
you’re running the semi-edited version through?

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:03.229
Jennifer Brody: &nbsp; I don’t really have a
focus group.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.009
&nbsp; I think that when you first start writing,
a lot of authors have critique partners which

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:11.610
are really important to the process which
would be trusted readers which you trust to

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.270
give feedback on your manuscript.&nbsp;

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:18.110
I have another author which I trade books
with and he writes stuff totally different

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.580
from me.&nbsp;

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:27.140
Typically these days it’s non-fiction, WWII
Nazi books, so they are totally different.&nbsp;

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.890
But he’s really smart and we trade reads.&nbsp;

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.230
Now that I have a publishing deal and that
I have a big publishing team, I don’t do

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.940
that as much and I don’t really have the
time.&nbsp;

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:42.670
So my team of people who are reading and giving
feedback are my agent and my editors at my

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.700
publishing house so and at this point I am
really tailoring the books to them because

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.380
that is who is going to be distributing my
book.&nbsp;

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.562
And at this point I am really trying to keep
a consistency in the story and I kind of know

00:02:55.562 --> 00:02:59.790
where I am and how I am writing it, so I’m
not at a place where I need feedback so much

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.500
on how I am writing, if that makes sense?&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.350
In the beginning a lot of it is trying to
improve the craft.&nbsp;

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.340
I’m at a point now where I am really trying
to tell the best stories that I can because

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.680
I already have a style that I’ve developed
in how I write.&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.040
So I am looking forward to writing something
different and new and I’m sure I’ll be

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.180
using critique partners on that once I start
writing my next book.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:21.430
Question for the Viewers: &nbsp; Do you read YA
Fiction?

